From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: Intellectual Property, ppl, etc.

T. David Burns writes, responding to David Friedman:
>1 is still a wash. 2 and 3 are net gains. 4 is a [tiny] net loss. But note
>that to A, 1 is a loss, 2 a wash, 3 is a tiny gain, and 4 is a loss. Why
>would A agree to this change in the arrangement? 

If the total net for A & B is positive, but A's net is negative, then
a deal can be struck as long as B offers to pay A enough extra cash.

>Since most of the costs and benefits of intellectual property get
>shifted to the publishers, they are in a good position to make the
>efficient decision. There is a potential collective action problem, in
>that the beneficiaries of intellectual property may not form an
>association

I think anarchy intrinsically imagines such a fragmentation of power
that such collective action problems become simply insurmountable.
Anarchy may thus just lose on intellectual property, but it may also
prevent collective action by protection agencies to enslave their
customers.  That is, it may prevent states.  A reasonable tradeoff I say.

Robin Hanson

