Date: Mon, 30 Aug 1993 12:31:40 -0700
From: dkrieger@Synopsys.COM (Dave Krieger)
Subject: REVIEW: Extropaganza a definite success

I think all who attended will agree that the Extropy 5th Anniversary party
at Mark Desilet's place in Boulder Creek was a smashing success.  A
trans-party.  A Jupiter-sized-brain-bending cataclysm of enjoyment and
intellectual stimulation. In fact, I speak presumptuously in using the past
tense; when I left Sunday morning, a large contingent were on their way out
to a local restaurant for breakfast.  They may still be partying somewhere,
much like the permanent floating party in "Life, The Universe, and
Everything."  Missing; Presumed Fed.

Highlights:

* Our affable hosts Mark Desilets and his lovely helpmeet Lenore put on a
spectacular spread of delectable comestibles.  To my knowledge, nobody went
hungry.  The excellent setting, featuring vistas of glorious pine-covered
hills and a popular fixture that served both as a "cool tub" in the
afternoon heat and a "hot tub" in the cool of the evening, combined to give
a sense of escape from the dull world of statism, deathism, and other
anti-extropianisms.

* Robin Hanson's talk on the economics of private law enforcement was so
well-attended that it almost shut down the rest of the party (until the
lecture room could accomodate no more people, and new arrivals were forced
to mingle with the rest of us instead).  The discussion was animated and
engaging, and its ideas were still being ramificated many hours later.

* The auditory environment was enhanced by an impromptu jam session on the
patio deck; featured musicians included Anton Sherwood on drum, Derek Ryan
on guitar, and Eric S. Raymond on flute.  The visual environment was
likewise enhanced by the raiment of E-babe Romana Machado, who attended the
party dressed as The State, accompanied by her housemate Geoff Dale,
dressed as a Taxpayer.

* Pat Salsbury demonstrated both his engineering genius and one product
thereof, in a tubside seminar so idea-packed it set many Extropian minds
buzzing.  Though Pat is a Trained Professional, I fear most of his
listeners are still going to Try This At Home.

* There was performance art in the living room for much of the evening, as
some folks whose names I didn't catch performed slow-motion gymnastics in
time with spacey music.

* A contingent of Mark's co-workers dropped by later on, enabling those of
us with an evangelistic spirit to amuse both themselves and Mark's pals
through discussion of the giant-sized printout of the Principles displayed
on one wall of the living room.

* I didn't make it to sunrise; with 30 other pooped party-goers, I zonked
out in Mark's living room, which came to resemble one of those refugee
shelters they set up in high-school gymnasiums after natural disasters. 
Those who stayed up all night (limited, I believe, to Pat Salsbury, R.
Bradford Dillon, and Perry Metzger) said that the sunrise was appropriately
spectacular.

* Those capable of alertness at 7:00 a.m. after so much fun the night
before demonstrated true Extropian spirit by counteracting the forces of
entropy and cleaning up the party site.  I felt horribly guilty as I lay on
the living room floor watching.

Obviously, as today's low traffic indicates, most of the Extropian world is
still partying somewhere, or perhaps rehabilitating.  If you were at the
party and are reading this, can you think of anything I left out?

