From: phoenix@ugcs.caltech.edu (La Sundial Vermin)
Subject: WAR/NANO/LAW:  Vingian Future

I'll avoid the direct nanarchy debate, but I'll take the opportunity to try
something else.  Take a situation similar to that in _Marooned in
Realtime_, with some small number of very high-tech spacers roughly similar
to those in that book, but more so.  I'm not sure the existence of bobbles
is that important, the essential being that they have advanced aerospace
capability, efficient ways of getting energy, and weapons (and other)
production facilities.  Plus two things Vinge left out: the capability to
produce autons, and advanced biotech, probably with large stocks of Earth
DNA or embryos.  Capable of growing human beings, in variety, or
terraforming a planet.  I don't see the presence of low-techs as at all
relevant to thsi scenario.

What do other extropians think would happen in such a situation?  Note that
the biggest restraining factor on Vinge's spacers, the finite number of
autons, has been removed.  With a small number it would probably depend on
the personalities and experiences of the people.  I like to think that a
group who had achieved this state together, and had been friendly (perhaps
the last survivors of the spacer rebellion against (radioactive) Earth)
would continue to be semi-cooperative and friendly, perhaps marrying and
having children; but perhaps not, after a time.  And if they didn't know
each other, or if there were a lot... they can create any material thing
they can design, limited only by raw supplies.  So would there be a race
for remote homesteading, possibly knocking each other off?  A spontaneous
development of a market a mutual respect for each other's claims?  Apathy?
"You play god in this galaxy, I'll take the one next door?"  Or would the
need for other people's ideas and algorithms play a sufficient binding
role, if they weren't all friends from the beginning?

